00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.660
Next comes stall.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:45.600
Stole.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:46.700
It means table.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.460
Книга лежит на столе.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.320
The book is lying on the table.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.160
Stole sounds like stool.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.140
But it names the flat surface where you eat or work.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.360
Repeat after me.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.100
Stole.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.620
Stole comes next.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:05.620
Сту, it means chair, сиди на стуле, сит он дочер, и triumphs в сту, which makes sense for the

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:13.400
sun you sit on, репит авторми, сту, кровать means bed, кровать, я сплю в кровати, I sleep in the

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.720
bed, пикчероковый закреп, оркот варью рест от Найд, репит авторми, кровать, лампа из

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:26.740
our wordful lamp, лампа, лампа светит в комнате, the lamp shines in the room, it sounds exactly like the

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:27.460
English Lamp.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.780
So it is easy to remember, repeat after me, lamp.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.360
Paul means floor.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.080
Paul.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:34.580
Кот лежит на полу.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.240
The cat is lying on the floor.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.820
Paul sounds like Paul.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.320
Yet it names the ground under your feet at home.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:41.600
Repeat after me.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:42.180
Paul.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.220
Patholog is the word for ceiling.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:45.440
По-толок.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.760
Patholog белый.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:47.960
Da ceiling is white.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.840
Sync of the top of the room that logs the walls together.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.180
Repeat after me.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.160
По-толок.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.920
Файно ли выхав в ванное?

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.220
Ван-на-я, it means bass room, я мойсь в ванной, I wash in the bass room.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.940
Ван-на-я, sounds like a vent full of water for basing.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.180
Репит авторми, ван-на-я.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.640
In Russian, we use the preposition to mean in when we talk about being inside the room.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.500
It works like the English in, yet the noun often ends within E or E sound.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.940
Я в комнате, I am in the room.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.620
Кот в ванной, the cat is in the bass room.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:21.860
в доме.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.120
Мы в доме.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.920
Теперь вы можете сказать, где-то или что-то есть.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.060
В мене русские партминты вы увидите хови-вудын-дорс и цикт-куртенцовы

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.440
в виндозь, чтобы убить виндозь в виндозь.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.860
Раши often say дом это крепость, which means home is a fortress.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.480
It shows how important a warm, safe home feels after cold months outside.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:47.100
Before we practice, here is something really cool about one of today's words.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.860
окно стартет long ago as ok, the old Slavic word for I.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.760
People saw the window as the eye of the house that lets you look out.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.380
The word kept that spirit through the centuries and became a kno.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.540
It looks nothing like the English window, yet both grew from the same idea of an opening

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.200
to see through.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.260
Picture your window as a giant friendly eye on the wall of your home.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.860
Do you remember what a kno means, say it with me, or k no window.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:24.700
На утро, using this words at home today, Draw a room and label the window door and table, or walk through your house and point to three objects while saying the Russian names.

